[examples]
.PP
Each case below is also a runnable test case in the sge sources:
.br
.UR https://github.com/DBinvent/sge/tree/main/examples
.UE
.PP
Options go on the command line, or into a config file given with \fB\-c\fR:
one \fIname\fR=\fIvalue\fR per line, the name without its leading \fB\-\-\fR,
and \fB#\fR for a comment.
The command line wins over the file, and an option set in neither is read
from the environment, by its name in upper case: \fBDB_NAME\fR for
\fB\-\-db_name\fR.
.SS "1. Migrate a database"
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg migrate
.EE
.PP
with \fIapp.cfg\fR:
.PP
.EX
db_host=localhost
db_name=app
db_user=app
db_password=secret
source_dir=./sql
.EE
.PP
sge reads the scripts in \fB\-\-source_dir\fR, sub\-directories included
(\fB\-\-source_recur=no\fR stops that), and applies them version by version,
recording each one in the \fIsge_history\fR table.
A file name carries the script type, the version and a name:
\fIV1_2__orders.sql\fR is version 1.2.
Within a version the types run in this order:
.TP
.B P
pre\-deploy SQL, run once
.TP
.B S
schema YAML (12): compared with the database, and the difference applied;
its data loads with it (11)
.TP
.B V
versioned SQL, run once
.TP
.B R
re\-runnable SQL, run again whenever its file changes
.TP
.B after
\fIafter_each.sql\fR after each version and \fIafter.sql\fR after the whole
migration, every time and not recorded (\fB\-\-after_each\fR and
\fB\-\-after\fR name them)
.PP
A P or V script fails the migration when its file changed after it ran;
\fB\-\-checksum=no\fR turns that check off.
.SS "2. See the SQL first"
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-dry_run=yes \-\-dry_run_to=plan.sql migrate
.EE
.PP
Writes every statement to \fIplan.sql\fR and runs none of them, except
\fBCREATE DATABASE\fR and \fBCREATE ROLE\fR when \fB\-\-createdb\fR is set.
\fB\-\-dry_run\fR takes a value: given alone, it would take the next word,
\fBmigrate\fR, as that value.
.SS "3. Create the database and its owner"
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-createdb=yes \-\-createdb_user=postgres \e
    \-\-createdb_pwd=secret prepare
.EE
.PP
Connects as \fB\-\-createdb_user\fR, creates the role \fB\-\-db_user\fR and
the database \fB\-\-db_name\fR when they are missing, and creates the history
table.
\fBprepare\fR runs no script; \fB\-\-createdb\fR works with \fBmigrate\fR too.
.SS "4. Migrate up to a version"
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-target=1 migrate
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-target=2 migrate
.EE
.PP
The first run stops after version 1, the second takes the database on to
version 2.
Without \fB\-\-target\fR, whose default is \fB*\fR, sge applies every version
it finds.
.SS "5. Connect from the environment or by URL"
.EX
DB_HOST=localhost DB_NAME=app DB_USER=app DB_PASSWORD=secret sge prepare
sge \-\-db_url=postgresql://app:secret@localhost:5432/app migrate
.EE
.SS "6. Scripts from git"
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-source_git=https://example.com/team/db.git \e
    \-\-source_git_ref=v1.4 \-\-source_git_dir=sql migrate
.EE
.PP
Clones the repository into a local cache (\fB\-\-source_git_cache\fR), checks
out \fIv1.4\fR and reads the scripts from its \fIsql\fR directory.
The commit it resolved is recorded as each script's tag, unless
\fB\-\-script_tag\fR is set.
.SS "7. Where the history goes, and how a run is labelled"
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-schema=ops \-\-table=deploy_log \e
    \-\-script_tag=release\-42 \-\-executed_by=ci migrate
.EE
.PP
Keeps the history in \fIops.deploy_log\fR instead of \fIpublic.sge_history\fR,
and stamps each script this run applies with the tag and the executor;
\fB\-\-db_target\fR and \fB\-\-db_catalog\fR label it further.
.SS "8. The history in another database"
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-logdb_name=ops migrate
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-logdb=primary \-\-logdb_name=ops migrate
.EE
.PP
The first writes the history to \fIops\fR as well as to \fIapp\fR
(\fB\-\-logdb=copy\fR, the default); the second writes it to \fIops\fR only.
A \fB\-\-logdb_\fR\fIxxx\fR option left unset takes its \fB\-\-db_\fR\fIxxx\fR
value.
.SS "9. What is deployed"
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg info
.EE
.PP
Prints, by \fB\-\-db_target\fR and \fB\-\-db_catalog\fR, the version each
database is at, whether the last run succeeded, its tag and who ran it: as
YAML, or as JSON with \fB\-\-snapshot_format=json\fR.
.SS "10. Snapshot a database"
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-snapshot_to=./schema snapshot
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-snapshot_format=json \-\-snapshot_to=schema.json snapshot
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-snapshot_data=shop.customer \e
    \-\-snapshot_to=./schema snapshot
.EE
.PP
Writes the database schema in the S file format (12), or as JSON; with
\fB\-\-snapshot_data\fR, the rows of the tables it lists go to CSV files too,
ready for an ETL load (11).
Not every column property is read back from the catalog, and some types,
arrays among them, are not supported, so a snapshot can differ from the YAML
it was deployed from.
.SS "11. Load data with the schema (ETL)"
.PP
A table in an S file can name a CSV file, with a header row, to load into it:
\fBdata_file: D1__customer.csv\fR.
\fB\-\-etl\fR picks the rule, for the tables \fB\-\-snapshot_data\fR lists or,
without it, for all of them:
.TP
.B apply
the default: insert the rows whose key is not in the table yet
.TP
.B force
insert, and update the rows whose key is already there
.TP
.B clean
as force, and delete the rows the file does not have
.TP
.B ignore
as apply, but a missing file is no error
.TP
.B skip
load nothing
.PP
.EX
sge \-c app.cfg \-\-etl=clean \-\-snapshot_data=shop.customer \e
    \-\-snapshot_to=./data migrate
.EE
.PP
Loads \fIshop.customer\fR by the clean rule and every other table by apply,
reading the CSV files from \fI./data\fR.
.SS "12. A schema file"
.PP
\fIS1__shop.yaml\fR declares two tables in the SchemaGuard YAML format.
sge creates what is missing and alters what differs, and drops nothing the
file merely leaves out:
.PP
.EX
database:
  \- schemaName: shop
    tables:
      \- table:
          tableName: customer
          description: who buys
          columns:
            \- column:
                name: id
                type: serial
                constraint:
                  primaryKey: true
            \- column:
                name: email
                type: varchar(250)
                constraint:
                  nullable: false
                index:
                  unique: true
      \- table:
          tableName: orders
          columns:
            \- column:
                name: id
                type: serial
                constraint:
                  primaryKey: true
            \- column:
                name: customer_id
                type: int
                constraint:
                  foreignKey:
                    references: shop.customer
            \- column:
                name: created_on
                type: timestamp
                defaultValue: now()
.EE
.PP
\fBreferences\fR names a \fIschema.table\fR, and the key points at its primary
key.
The format is described in the SchemaGuard YAML reference, and every field it
accepts is in its validation schema:
.br
.UR https://github.com/vkrinitsyn/schema_guard/blob/main/yaml.md
.UE
.br
.UR https://github.com/vkrinitsyn/schema_guard/blob/main/src/schema.yaml
.UE
.PP
\fBsge help migrate\fR and \fBsge help\fR describe every option.
